#### Setting default user and password

If you wish to change the default username and password of `guest` / `guest`, you can do so with the `RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER` and `RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS` environmental variables. These variables were available previously in the docker-specific entrypoint shell script but are now available in RabbitMQ directly.

```console
$ appjail oci run -Pd \
    -o overwrite=force \
    -o virtualnet=":<random> default" \
    -o nat \
    -o template=template.conf \
    -o expose="15672" \
    -o expose="5672" \
    -e RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_USER=user \
    -e RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_PASS=password \
    ghcr.io/appjail-makejails/rabbitmq:%%BASE1%%-management some-rabbit
```

You can then go to `http://some-rabbit:15672` (from the same host) or `http://host-ip:15672` (from external hosts) in a browser and use `user`/`password` to gain access to the [management UI](https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/management).

#### Setting default vhost

If you wish to change the default vhost, you can do so with the `RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_VHOST` environmental variables:

```console
$ appjail oci run -Pd \
    -o overwrite=force \
    -o virtualnet=":<random> default" \
    -o nat \
    -o template=template.conf \
    -e RABBITMQ_DEFAULT_VHOST=my_vhost \
    ghcr.io/appjail-makejails/rabbitmq:%%BASE1%%-management some-rabbit
```

#### Memory Limits

In AppJail, you can set [limits](https://appjail.readthedocs.io/en/latest/limits/), and in this case, one for `vmemoryuse`.

```console
$ appjail oci run -Pd \
    -o overwrite=force \
    -o virtualnet=":<random> default" \
    -o nat \
    -o template=template.conf \
    -o limits="vmemoryuse:deny=4g" \
    ghcr.io/appjail-makejails/rabbitmq some-rabbit
```

However, unlike Linux, where RabbitMQ could calculate `vm_memory_high_watermark.absolute` (described under [Memory Alarms](https://www.rabbitmq.com/memory.html)) using cgroups, there is no equivalent in FreeBSD, so this parameter must be set explicitly.

```console
$ appjail oci run -Pd \
    -o overwrite=force \
    -o virtualnet=":<random> default" \
    -o nat \
    -o template=template.conf \
    -o limits="vmemoryuse:deny=5g" \
    -o fstab="$PWD/30-high_watermark.conf usr/local/etc/rabbitmq/conf.d/30-high_watermark.conf" \
    ghcr.io/appjail-makejails/rabbitmq some-rabbit
$ appjail oci exec -u noroot some-rabbit rabbitmqctl status | grep -Ee "^Memory high watermark setting: "
Memory high watermark setting: 4.295 gb, computed to: 4.295 gb
```

**30-high_watermark.conf**:

```
vm_memory_high_watermark.absolute = 4Gi
```

#### Erlang Cookie

See the [RabbitMQ "Clustering Guide"](https://www.rabbitmq.com/clustering.html#erlang-cookie) for more information about cookies and why they're necessary. For setting a consistent cookie (especially useful for clustering but also for remote/cross-container administration via `rabbitmqctl`), provide a cookie file (default location of `/var/db/rabbitmq/.erlang.cookie`).

#### Management Plugin

There is a second set of tags provided with the [management plugin](https://www.rabbitmq.com/management.html) installed and enabled by default, which is available on the standard management port of 15672, with the default username and password of `guest` / `guest`:

```console
$ appjail oci run -Pd \
    -o overwrite=force \
    -o virtualnet=":<random> default" \
    -o nat \
    -o template=template.conf \
    ghcr.io/appjail-makejails/rabbitmq:%%BASE1%%-management some-rabbit
```

You can access it by visiting `http://some-rabbit:15672` in a browser or, if you need access outside the host, on port 8080:

```console
$ appjail oci run -Pd \
    -o overwrite=force \
    -o virtualnet=":<random> default" \
    -o nat \
    -o template=template.conf \
    -o expose="8080:15672" \
    ghcr.io/appjail-makejails/rabbitmq:%%BASE1%%-management some-rabbit
```

You can then go to `http://some-rabbit:15672` (from the same host) or `http://host-ip:8080` (from external hosts) in a browser.

#### Enabling Plugins

Creating a Containerfile will have them enabled at runtime. To see the full list of plugins present on the image `rabbitmq-plugins list`.

```dockerfile
FROM ghcr.io/appjail-makejails/rabbitmq:%%BASE1%%-management
RUN rabbitmq-plugins enable --offline rabbitmq_mqtt rabbitmq_federation_management rabbitmq_stomp
```

You can also mount a file at `/usr/local/etc/rabbitmq/enabled_plugins` with contents as an erlang list of atoms ending with a period.

Example `enabled_plugins`:

```
[rabbitmq_federation_management,rabbitmq_management,rabbitmq_mqtt,rabbitmq_stomp].
```

#### Additional Configuration

If configuration is required, it is recommended to supply an appropriate `/usr/local/etc/rabbitmq/rabbitmq.conf` file (see [the "Configuration File(s)" section of the RabbitMQ documentation for more details](https://www.rabbitmq.com/configure.html#configuration-files)), for example via nullfs-mount, or a short `Containerfile` with a `COPY` instruction.

Alternatively, it is possible to use the `RABBITMQ_SERVER_ADDITIONAL_ERL_ARGS` environment variable, whose syntax is described [in section 7.8 ("Configuring an Application") of the Erlang OTP Design Principles User's Guide](http://erlang.org/doc/design_principles/applications.html#id81887) (the appropriate value for `-ApplName` is `-rabbit`), this method requires a slightly different reproduction of its equivalent entry in `rabbitmq.conf`. For example, configuring [`channel_max`](https://www.rabbitmq.com/configure.html#config-items) would look something like `-e RABBITMQ_SERVER_ADDITIONAL_ERL_ARGS="-rabbit channel_max 4007"`. Where the space between the variable `channel_max` and its value `4007` correctly becomes a comma when translated in the environment.
